/**
@page reading Reading fields

Fields are read through getters, the read half of accessor-mediated access
(writing is in @ref modifying). A getter performs the bounds check and the
byte-order conversion.

@section reading_get Getters

A getter takes the view and a pointer to the output and returns a status code. A
non-zero status means the read failed, for example on a truncated packet:

@code{.c}
SEC("xdp")
int low_ttl_drop(struct xdp_md *ctx)
{
    bnt_eth  eth;
    bnt_ipv4 ip;
    __u8 ttl;

    if (bnt_eth__parse(ctx, 0, &eth))  return XDP_PASS;
    if (bnt_eth__next_ipv4(&eth, &ip)) return XDP_PASS;
    if (bnt_ipv4__get_ttl(&ip, &ttl))  return XDP_PASS;

    return ttl <= 1 ? XDP_DROP : XDP_PASS;
}
@endcode

On failure a getter zeroes its output. Check the status where a failed read
would change the outcome.

@section reading_byteorder Byte order

Getters return fields in host byte order. Addresses are the exception: they stay
in network byte order, because addresses are compared and copied in their
on-wire form rather than used as numbers:

@code{.c}
__u16  dport;
__be32 saddr;

bnt_tcp__get_dport(&tcp, &dport);    /* host order:    dport == 23                */
bnt_ipv4__get_saddr(&ip, &saddr);   /* network order: saddr == bpf_htonl(0x0A000001) */
@endcode
*/
